  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Subletting can be a great deal for everyone involved. You stop paying rent on an empty apartment while you are away for the summer, a semester, or a work assignment, and someone else gets a furnished place for a few months without signing a year-long lease. But a sublet done carelessly can put your lease, your security deposit, and your relationship with your landlord at risk.</p>
<p>The good news is that doing it properly is mostly a matter of reading carefully, asking in writing, and keeping good records. This guide walks through the process step by step.</p>
<p><em>This article is general information, not legal advice. Rules differ by state, city, and building, and your lease is the starting point. When in doubt, check your lease and local law, or talk to a local tenant organization or attorney.</em></p>

<h2>Step 1: Read your lease, all of it</h2>
<p>Before you post a listing or tell a friend your place is available, pull out your lease and look for anything about subletting, assignment, guests, occupancy, or short-term rentals. These clauses are often buried in a section called "Use of Premises," "Assignment and Subletting," or "Occupants." Common versions include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Subletting prohibited.</strong> The lease says plainly that you may not sublet. Some local laws limit how strictly a landlord can enforce this, but you should assume the clause applies unless you have confirmed otherwise.</li>
<li><strong>Subletting allowed with written consent.</strong> This is very common. You can sublet, but only after the landlord approves in writing. Some leases add that consent "will not be unreasonably withheld."</li>
<li><strong>Silent.</strong> The lease says nothing at all. Do not take silence as permission. Local law may fill the gap, and other clauses, such as limits on who may occupy the unit, can still apply.</li>
<li><strong>Guest limits.</strong> Many leases cap how long a guest can stay, often a couple of weeks. A "long-term guest" paying you rent is usually a subtenant, not a guest, no matter what you call them.</li>
</ul>
<p>Also look for fees. Some landlords charge an application or processing fee for a sublet request, or require the subtenant to pass the same screening as a new tenant.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Check local rules and building rules</h2>
<p>Subletting is governed by more than your lease. Some states and cities have specific statutes about a tenant's right to sublet, how a landlord must respond to a request, and what counts as a reasonable refusal. New York, for example, has a well-known statute covering sublets in certain buildings. Other places say very little and leave it to the lease.</p>
<p>Separately, many cities regulate <strong>short-term rentals</strong>, which are often defined as stays shorter than 30 days. If you are thinking of renting your place for a few days or weeks at a time, you may run into registration requirements, occupancy taxes, or outright bans that do not apply to a three-month sublet. Our guide to <a href="/blog/short-term-rental-laws-what-hosts-should-know/">short-term rental laws</a> covers the distinction in more detail.</p>
<p>Finally, if you live in a condo, co-op, or building with an HOA, there may be bylaws that restrict rentals or require board approval. Rent-stabilized, rent-controlled, and subsidized housing often comes with extra rules too, including limits on what you can charge a subtenant.</p>
<p>Good places to check: your city or county housing department website, your state attorney general's consumer pages, and local tenant unions or legal aid organizations.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Ask your landlord in writing</h2>
<p>Even if your lease seems to allow subletting, asking first is the single best way to protect yourself. A friendly, organized request makes approval much more likely. Send it by email or letter (whatever your lease specifies for notices) and include:</p>
<ol>
<li>Your name, unit, and the dates you plan to be away.</li>
<li>The proposed subtenant's name and contact information, if you have found someone.</li>
<li>The reason for the sublet (summer internship, travel assignment, semester abroad).</li>
<li>A statement that you will remain responsible for rent and for the unit under your lease.</li>
<li>An offer to share the subtenant's references or have them complete the landlord's application.</li>
<li>Your forwarding contact details while you are away.</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep a copy of what you sent and any reply. If the landlord says yes, ask for the approval in writing, ideally naming the subtenant and the dates. A verbal "sure, that's fine" from a property manager is easy to forget or dispute months later.</p>
<p>If the landlord says no, ask why. Sometimes the concern is easy to address, such as wanting to screen the subtenant or meet them first. If you believe the refusal conflicts with your lease or local law, that is a good moment to talk to a tenant organization rather than going ahead anyway.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Screen your subtenant carefully and fairly</h2>
<p>In most sublet arrangements, you remain on the hook to your landlord. If your subtenant stops paying, damages the unit, or disturbs neighbors, the landlord will usually come to you. That makes screening important.</p>
<ul>
<li>Talk by video or meet in person before agreeing to anything.</li>
<li>Ask about their reason for needing housing, their dates, and their work or school situation.</li>
<li>Ask for references, such as a previous landlord or an employer.</li>
<li>Be clear about house rules, building rules, and anything unusual about the apartment.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apply the same criteria to everyone. Fair housing laws apply to many sublets, and you should never accept or reject someone based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, familial status, or any other characteristic protected under federal, state, or local law. Focus on things that actually matter: whether they can pay, whether the dates work, and whether they will follow the lease rules.</p>
<p>If you list on The Sublet, renters contact you through a relay form, so your personal email address is not exposed to everyone who views your listing. You can have a few conversations before deciding who to share more details with.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Put it in a written sublease</h2>
<p>A handshake is not enough. A written sublease agreement sets expectations and gives both of you something to point to if a disagreement comes up. At minimum it should cover the parties, the dates, rent and due dates, the security deposit, utilities, house rules, and how the arrangement can end early. It should also reference your master lease and state that the subtenant agrees to follow it.</p>
<p>We cover this in detail in <a href="/blog/sublease-agreement-what-to-include/">what to include in a sublease agreement</a>. Attach a copy of the landlord's written consent if you have it.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Document the apartment's condition</h2>
<p>Before your subtenant moves in, walk through the apartment and take date-stamped photos and video of every room, including floors, walls, appliances, and furniture. Write a short condition report and have both of you sign it. Do the same walkthrough together at move-out. This protects your deposit with the landlord and gives you a fair basis for handling the subtenant's deposit. Our guide to <a href="/blog/security-deposits-for-sublets/">security deposits for sublets</a> covers how to handle that money.</p>

<h2>Step 7: Handle money cleanly</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep paying your landlord on time.</strong> Your obligation usually does not pause because your subtenant is late.</li>
<li><strong>Use traceable payment methods</strong> with your subtenant and keep records of every payment.</li>
<li><strong>Be careful about charging more than you pay.</strong> Some leases and some local laws, especially for regulated apartments, limit what you can charge a subtenant. Furnishing can justify a modest premium in some places but not others.</li>
<li><strong>Prorate fairly</strong> for partial months. The <a href="/tools/proration/">proration calculator</a> makes this simple.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 8: Stay reachable</h2>
<p>Give your subtenant and your landlord a reliable way to reach you, and check in occasionally. Let your subtenant know who to call for maintenance emergencies. Small problems handled quickly stay small.</p>

<h2>A quick checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>Read your lease for sublet, assignment, guest, and occupancy clauses.</li>
<li>Check city, state, and building rules, including short-term rental rules.</li>
<li>Request landlord consent in writing and keep the reply.</li>
<li>Screen subtenants consistently and in line with fair housing law.</li>
<li>Sign a written sublease that references the master lease.</li>
<li>Document condition at move-in and move-out.</li>
<li>Keep paying rent on time and keep payment records.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ready to find someone? You can <a href="/post-a-sublet/">post a sublet</a> once you have permission sorted out. Free listings are reviewed before they go live, and the process takes just a few minutes.</p>
